Module — the Zero Framework skeleton module
===========================================

A live, routable reference module at /module/ (it ships in core/modules/ and is
switched on in app/modules.ini).

Every directory below is a worked example. `mkmod` (in this directory) copies
from here to scaffold new modules, so this is also the template source. Edit a
file here and mkmod's output changes with it.


Framework-aware — core/ reads these
-----------------------------------

  Module.php            Controller. Class name must match the directory name.
                        namespace Zero\Module; extends \Zero\Core\Module.

  view/                 $this->viewPath. Endpoint templates; $this is in scope.
                        view/errorNNN.php is used by Response::renderError().

  frame/                $this->framePath. Overrides the global head/header/
                        footer/sideNav. If you override head.php you MUST call
                        getStylesheets(), getScripts(), and getComponents() or
                        asset auto-loading silently stops. Submodules with no
                        frame/ inherit the nearest ancestor's by walking up the
                        directory tree — this walk-up is unique to frame/;
                        asset directories are resolved per-module only, with
                        no such inheritance.

  model/                $this->modelPath. Autoloaded, but ONLY for classes whose
                        namespace contains \Model\ (see core/Response.php).

  lib/                  Classes other modules call: namespace
                        Zero\Module\{Name}\Lib, loaded by Application::
                        classFile() (Lib -> lib/). Other modules use a module's
                        lib/, never its model/. See "Sharing logic between
                        modules: lib/" in ZERO.md, the framework internals
                        doc. This module has none.

  assets/css/           Auto-loaded by filename. {module}.css loads on every
  assets/js/            endpoint; {endpoint}.css loads on that endpoint only.
                        Symlinked to /assets/{module}/ on first request.

  assets/component/     ShadowComponent HTML. Response::getComponents() scans
                        this on every request. NOT a top-level component/ dir.
                        defineBasePaths() does set $this->componentPath, but
                        nothing reads it — getComponents() resolves through
                        moduleAssetDir instead. Don't rely on it.

  config.ini            Module-scope constants, defined for this request only.
  config/*.ini          Same, one file per concern. Both are parsed by
                        Application::defineConstants().
  config-tmpl/          A copy of each config file with the site's values
                        blanked. config.ini and config/ are never committed;
                        config-tmpl/ is. Installing a module on a site means
                        copying these into place and filling them in.

  strings/*.ini         The module's wording defaults: every key becomes a constant
                        (prefix the module's name in upper snake case: AUTH_,
                        ACCESS_REQUEST_), defined only if the site's
                        app/strings/*.ini has not defined it. Use the constant in
                        views, never the text. Loaded when the module is built
                        (a submodule loads its parent's) by Strings::loadModule();
                        code that needs another module's wording calls
                        Strings::loadModule('Name') first. A module that is off
                        loads none. strings/ is committed.
                        Files are read with INI_SCANNER_RAW. Wrap the whole value
                        in double quotes; inside them ' = ; and " are literal and
                        need no escaping. Unwrapped, ; starts a comment and
                        surrounding spaces are dropped.
                            KEY="We'll email you a link; it says "hi" = done."

  aux/                  aux/name.php is included by $this->getAuxiliary('name').
                        You do NOT call this yourself: the global frame at
                        app/frontend/frame/header.php calls getAuxiliary("nav")
                        for whichever module is handling the request, so simply
                        creating aux/nav.php injects the fragment into the nav.

  submodule/             Module::__call() resolves these when the parent has no
                        matching method. Infinitely nestable. The namespace is
                        the parent's full name, with no "submodule" segment.
                        A submodule frame/ containing only head.php still gets
                        the global header/sideNav/footer, via Response::add()'s
                        VIEW_PATH fallback for the pieces it doesn't override.

  Admin/                AdminResponse::getAdminModules() walks Modules::all().
                        A module is listed when its {Module}Admin class exists,
                        the module is switched on (app/modules.ini [enabled])
                        and the user holds every key in `permissions`.
                        admin-config.php supplies label/icon/description/
                        permissions; the card's place comes from the [order]
                        section of app/modules.ini. Admin assets are inlined,
                        never symlinked public — auto mode (which inlines this
                        module's own Admin/assets/*.css automatically) is
                        preferred over calling loadAdminStyles() by hand.
                        Each discovered offering is one card in the "Modules"
                        section of the /admin dashboard. The skeleton is an
                        example module and is off unless app/modules.ini
                        turns it on.


Convention only — nothing in core/ reads these
----------------------------------------------

  helper/               Plain classes. Not autoloaded; require them yourself.
                        Singular. (Ttrpg/helper/ is the working example.)

  tests/                PHPUnit. phpunit.xml globs modules/*/tests (and
                        core/modules/*/tests for the bundled ones), so anything
                        here runs in the real suite. Extend Zero\Tests\Core\
                        ZeroTestCase. Run: vendor/bin/phpunit --testsuite Modules

  docs/                 Module-specific documentation.

  template/             Render fragments that are not endpoint views.

  data/                 Static fixture data shipped with the module.


Users, groups and permissions
-----------------------------

These belong to core, not to a module: Zero\Core\Session (who is making this
request), Zero\Core\Access\User, Zero\Core\Access\Group and Zero\Core\Access\Permission. A module
asks Session::userId() who is logged in. A module that shares its things with
groups or users builds a Permission with its own name and the thing's name,

    new Permission('inventory', 'item')             // key "inventory.item"

and checks "is the owner, or $perm->can($id, $userId, 'update')". It keeps no
permission table, writes no SQL against the group or permission tables, and
calls $perm->forget($id) when it deletes the thing. See core/PERMISSIONS.md.

A module never depends on the Admin module. It offers its admin screens in
Admin/ (above) and the Admin module picks them up if the site has it.


Deliberately not shown
----------------------

Some modules invent their own directories — sql/, storage/, scanner/, pics/,
bash/, mail-template/, adapters/, service/, archive/. These are one-module
inventions with no convention behind them. Invent your own freely; just don't
expect the framework to notice.


Creating a module
-----------------

  ./mkmod --help
  ./mkmod Inventory                    # minimal: controller, view, css, js, .gitignore
  ./mkmod Inventory --model --admin    # add model/ and Admin/
  ./mkmod Inventory --all --dry-run    # show the full tree, write nothing
  ./mkmod Inventory --submodule=Reorder   # nest a submodule
  ./mkmod                              # no name: prompt for each directory
  ./mkmod --selftest                   # verify mkmod against this skeleton

The name must be StudlyCaps. A new module is the site's: mkmod writes it into
the site's modules/ folder (the folder that holds core/, found from this
script's own location), never into core/. `--dest <dir>` writes into <dir>
instead, and `--dry-run` prints the destination. mkmod refuses to overwrite an
existing module unless you pass --force, refuses the name Module outright, and
refuses a name a bundled module in core/modules/ already has (compared the way
Modules::path() compares: case-insensitive, hyphens ignored). So
`./mkmod Auth` exits 1 — Auth ships in core — and so does a name that is
already a module in the destination folder.

Every module has its own .gitignore; mkmod copies this directory's. It ignores
config.ini and config/, so a generated module's config files are not committed
(mkmod prints a NOTE when this applies). Their blank copies in config-tmpl/
are. This skeleton keeps its own config examples tracked with negations that
sit in a MKMOD:SKELETON-ONLY block, so they stay here and a generated module
gets the plain file.

Run --selftest after editing anything in this directory. mkmod rewrites the
skeleton using patterns anchored to exact strings in these files; --selftest
turns a broken anchor into a loud failure rather than a silently wrong module.
